# TWO HEIGHTS SIDE BY SIDE, AND A RAISED PATCH SMALLER THAN THE DECK.
#
# M23 phase 1: the fixture that asks whether anything has to be BUILT for height
# to vary across the bridge as well as along it. The generator's whole vocabulary
# is one scalar per row -- `low[z]` is the height of the entire row -- so nothing
# in this game has ever produced either shape, and the claim that the renderer
# and the validator already handle them is a claim, not a fact.
#
# NO HOLES ANYWHERE. Every cell is solid, so anything that goes wrong here is
# about HEIGHT and cannot be blamed on the width work that came before it.
#
# WHAT IS IN HERE:
#
#   A LATERAL CLIFF. Rows 4-7, columns 0-5 stay at height 0 while columns 6-14
#   sit at height 2. That vertical face points ACROSS the screen rather than up
#   it, which is the orientation nothing in this game has ever drawn -- every
#   height change until now has been a horizontal line running the full width.
#
#   THE RAMP THAT MAKES IT LEGAL. Columns 6-7 climb one unit per row at rows 2
#   and 3. Without it the high half is deck nothing can reach, which
#   `_check_orphans` refuses outright -- and that refusal is itself worth
#   proving, because it is the rule a generated split plateau will have to
#   satisfy.
#
#   A TOWER. Rows 10-12, columns 11-13, two units above the deck around it,
#   reached by its own two-row ramp at column 12. A patch smaller than the row,
#   which is the footprint set-pieces cannot currently express.
#
#   THREE ROWS DEEP SO THE TEST CAN DISCRIMINATE, and the first draft was one.
#   A one-row tower has low ground directly behind it, so its underside drops
#   to the deck through a Z neighbour -- and an A/B that narrowed the eight-
#   neighbour scan to Z only left the tower reading a correct 2.00 while the
#   cliff fell to 1.00. The assertion was passing for the wrong reason. Three
#   rows put a cell in the MIDDLE with high ground both ways along the bridge,
#   so only its lateral neighbours can thicken it.
#
#   TWO RAMP ROWS, NOT ONE, and the first draft had one. A ramp climbs a single
#   unit per row and `_can_step` allows a rise only where the DESTINATION is a
#   ramp -- so one row cannot reach a height of two, and the tower came out as
#   six cells of marooned deck the validator correctly refused. The fixture was
#   wrong, not the rule, which is exactly what phase 1 is for.
#
# The entry and exit rows are flat and level so the segment joins a run normally.

name = test_split_height
base_height = 0
width = 15
length = 14
tags = fixture, split_height

